===============================================================================
 LINUX /etc/login.defs CONFIGURATION FILE
===============================================================================
1. OVERVIEW & PURPOSE

The /etc/login.defs file serves as the site-specific configuration file for 
the shadow password suite (util-linux / shadow tools). It defines system-wide 
defaults and security parameters used when creating, modifying, or deleting 
user accounts and groups via utilities such as useradd, usermod, userdel, 
groupadd, and chage.

Key Characteristics:
* File Ownership:   root:root
* Default Perms:    0644 (-rw-r--r--)
* Administrative Scope: Establishes global baseline settings for account creation,
  UID/GID range allocations, default password aging, and shell behavior.

Note: Parameters set in /etc/login.defs apply primarily at the time of account 
creation. Existing accounts are not automatically updated retroactively when this 
file is modified.


2. KEY CONFIGURATION PARAMETERS & STRUCTURE

The file is organized as a plain text key-value pair format:

PARAM_NAME    VALUE

--- A. Password Aging Defaults (Applied during useradd) ---

PASS_MAX_DAYS    90
Maximum number of days a password may be used before required reset.

PASS_MIN_DAYS    7
Minimum number of days required between password changes.

PASS_WARN_AGE    14
Number of days warning given to user prior to password expiration.


--- B. User ID (UID) and Group ID (GID) Allocation Ranges ---

UID_MIN          1000
UID_MAX          60000
Defines the range of User IDs available for assignment to regular users.

GID_MIN          1000
GID_MAX          60000
Defines the range of Group IDs available for assignment to regular groups.

SYS_UID_MIN      100
SYS_UID_MAX      999
Defines the reserved UID range for system accounts (e.g., daemons/services).

SYS_GID_MIN      100
SYS_GID_MAX      999
Defines the reserved GID range for system groups.


--- C. Environment & Login Shell Control ---

ENV_PATH         PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
Sets the standard PATH environment variable for regular users upon login.

ENV_SUPATH       PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
Sets the elevated PATH environment variable for superuser (root) sessions.

UMASK            022
Defines default file creation permission mask for newly created users. 
(022 results in standard 755 directory / 644 file permissions).

TTYGROUP         tty
TTYPERM          0600
Defines ownership and permissions for terminal devices upon user login.


--- D. Account Management & Cleanup Directives ---

CREATE_HOME      yes
Specifies whether useradd should create a home directory by default.

USERDEL_CMD      /usr/sbin/userdel-custom-script
Optional external script executed when removing a user with userdel.

DEFAULT_HOME     yes
Determines if user can log in if chdir to home directory fails.


--- E. Cryptographic Hashing Algorithms ---

ENCRYPT_METHOD   SHA512
Defines default hashing algorithm for user passwords (e.g., SHA512, YESCRYPT).

SHA_CRYPT_MIN_ROUNDS  5000
SHA_CRYPT_MAX_ROUNDS  10000
Defines minimum and maximum hashing round thresholds for SHA-256 / SHA-512.


3. OVERRIDING /etc/login.defs AT RUNTIME

Administrators can override parameters defined in /etc/login.defs dynamically 
during user or group creation using command-line switches.

Examples:

1. Temporary Override via `-K` Key-Value Flag (groupadd / useradd):
   $ sudo groupadd -K GID_MIN=5000 -K GID_MAX=6000 devteam
   $ sudo useradd -K PASS_MAX_DAYS=45 -m jdoe

2. Overriding via `/etc/default/useradd`:
   Specific user creation defaults (e.g., SHELL, HOME, SKEL) can be customized 
   separately in /etc/default/useradd using:
   $ sudo useradd -D -s /bin/bash


4. AUDITING & VERIFICATION COMMANDS

To inspect current system account settings derived from /etc/login.defs:

1. Check active user password aging attributes:
   $ sudo chage -l <username>

2. Audit next available UID/GID allocations:
   $ getent passwd | awk -F: '$3 >= 1000 {print $1, $3}' | sort -n -k2

3. Test configuration file syntax integrity:
   $ sudo pwck -r


5. SECURITY HARDENING RECOMMENDATIONS

1. Enforce Strict Password Rotation:
   Ensure PASS_MAX_DAYS does not exceed 90 days for standard enterprise compliance.

2. Restrict System File Permissions (UMASK):
   Set `UMASK 027` or `UMASK 077` in multi-tenant environments to restrict 
   unauthorized cross-user directory access.

3. Restrict Root Shell PATH:
   Verify ENV_SUPATH does not contain current directory pointers (`.`) to prevent 
   path-precedence binary execution attacks.
===============================================================================
